            <ArticleTitle>When should the diagnosis 'contact urticaria' be used?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>323-324</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/cod.12884</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In the dermatological literature, the term 'contact urticaria syndrome' is often used with patients presenting no symptoms of contact urticaria, but symptoms of adjacent mucous membranes evolving to a systemic allergic reaction. Contact urticaria refers to a wheal and flare reaction following external contact with a causative substance. Its use should be restricted to skin: a skin reaction after direct skin contact.</AbstractText>
